g the problem  even  faster and that's a good thing now what  made this problem  most most convenient was that juicy 0  in the middle of the problem sometimes  having a 1 in the middle of the problem  also makes things easier like in this  next problem  let's try 812 times three  we start on the left with three times  800 which is  2400. now go ahead and say the 2400  i know that 3 times 12 is going to be  under 2 400 is going to be under 100  so i can say 2400. next we do 3 times 12  which is  36 and there's your answer 2436.  here's another problem where you can say  the beginning of the answer  without any risk of having to correct  yourself it's when we have the number  five interacting with an even number  let's do 527 times eight  so here we have eight times five hundred  which is  four thousand go ahead and  say the four thousand there's no way  that what comes next  is going to make that four thousand  change into a five  thousand right because what comes next  is always less than ninety nine times  nine  that's less than a hundred times ten  that's less than a thousand so you can  safely say  four thousand get it out of your memory  now you do 27  times eight now that's a two by one  problem we can  we've simplified our life to a two by  one problem eight times  twenty is one sixty eight times seven is  fifty six  160 plus 56 is 216  and there's your answer 4216.  all right here's another easy one let's  do 925  eight all right so we'll start with  eight times  nine hundred which is seventy  two hundred now what what's coming next  after the seventy two hundred you can't  say the 7200  but look what's coming next 25 times 8.  now i happen to know that 25 times 8  is 200 right if i've got eight quarters  in my pocket  i know that that's two dollars and if  you if you know  if you know something don't be afraid to  use it eight times 25  is 200 add that to the 7200  and you have 7200 plus 200  is 7400 okay  let's try a harder problem let's try 789  times seven all right so we start with  seven  times seven hundred which is forty nine  hundred  seven times 80 is 560.  add those together from left to right  listen to the numbers  4 900 plus 560  is 5460  next we do seven times nine which is  sixty three  we're going to have to do fifty four  hundred sixty plus sixty three  now don't panic what you can notice  right now is that there's going to be a  carry  so relieve your memory by saying 5500  right now that 5400 becomes a 5500  now do 60 plus 63 that's as easy as 1 2  3 123 so you know how the answer ends  with 23. so there's your answer 5523  but there was another way of doing that  last  problem 789 times 7 that you might  find easier notice that 789  is 800 minus 11.  because of that we could do this problem  as seven times eight  what's 5600 minus 77  you know it's going to be 5500 and  something  5500 and what well the complement of 77  is 23 so there's your answer again  5523  as we've seen before we've taken a hard  addition problem  one that had lots of carries and turned  it into  an easy subtraction problem  once you've mastered two by one and  three by one multiplication  you can actually do most two by two  multiplication problems two digit times  two digit numbers  using what i call the factoring method  most two digit numbers can be factored  into smaller numbers as a matter of fact  69 of the 92-digit numbers more than  two-thirds of them  can be factored into smaller numbers and  we can often  take advantage of this for example  consider the problem 23 times 16.  now when i see 16 i can't resist  thinking of 16  as 8 times 2. so for me  when i do 23 times 16 i want to do 23  times 8 times 2. so that's going to be  our strategy first multiply by 8  then multiply by two you can even hold  that two on your fingers  on reserve if that helps you remember it  okay  let's do 23 times eight  all right 23 times eight that's a two by  one  eight times 20 is 160  8 times 3 is 24 160 plus 24  is 184 good pause take a break you're  halfway there  what do we do with that 184  we multiply it by the 2 you were holding  on to right  184 times 2. if i double 184  or use the 3x1 methods we've learned we  get 184 times  2 is 368. so our original problem  23 times 16 is 368.  by the way the reason that 23 times  8 times 2 is the same as 23 times 8  times 2 is due to what's known as the  associative law of multiplication  but let me show you another way to do 23  times 16.  i said 16 is 8 times 2 you could also  say  that 16 is 2 times 8 the same thing  not really because 23 times 16 we could  now do  as 23 times 2 times 8.  maybe you'll find that easier 23 times 2  is 46 and when you multiply that by 8  46 times 8 is 320 plus 48  is 368. this way you didn't have to do  any 3x1 multiplication  or maybe you'd rather think of 16 as  four times four 23 times 16  is 23 times four times four  23 times four that's a two by one eighty  plus twelve  is ninety two when you multiply that by  four  we have three sixty plus eight we get  three sixty eight okay let's do another  problem  suppose that your phone service costs  you 59  per month how much will that cost in a  year  all right so we can break up the number  12 lots of different ways  let's break it up as 6 times 2 so we'll  do  59 times 6 times  2 so we do 59 times 6 what's that  that's an easy two by one it's 300 plus  54  354 and now we multiply that  by 2. 354 by 2  is a not too bad 3 by 1 problem  because 2 times 350 do that all at once  is 700 and 2 times 4 is  8. so there's your answer 708  dollars one last problem  suppose a swimming pool has dimensions  14 feet  by 14 feet by seven feet deep  what's the volume of the pool okay well  the answer of course is 14 times 14  times 7  cubic feet let's do that multiplication  in our head  starting with 14 times 14  which we'll do a few different ways one  way  of doing that problem fourteen times  fourteen is to break fourteen into two  times seven  so now you have fourteen times two times  seven  which is twenty eight times seven and  twenty eight  7 that's 140 plus 56  is 196. another way of doing 14 times 14  is you could do 14 times 7  times 2. 14 times 7 is 98  and when you double 98 you get 196.  or we can use the method we learned in  lecture one  for multiplying two numbers between 10  and 20 right we're doing 14 times 14  let's not forget what we learned in  lecture one we can do that by taking  14 plus 4 which is 18  multiplying that by 10 to get 180  then add to that 4 times 4  16 to get 196.  okay so the original problem 14 times 14  times 7 becomes 196  times 7 which we can also do in a few  different ways  using the addition method we can do 196  times  7 by doing 7 times 100  hundred seven times ninety six hundred  thirty add those together to get  thirteen hundred  thirty then do seven times six forty two  add those together thirteen hundred  thirty plus 42  to get 1372 cubic feet  or you could do it by subtraction  you could take 196 times 7 and take  advantage of the fact  that 196 is 200 minus four  seven times two hundred is fourteen  hundred subtract seven times four  fourteen hundred minus twenty eight is  1300 and what the complement of 28  is 72 so there we have it again  1372 cubic feet  to summarize this lecture i've shown you  everything you need to know to do two by  one  and three by one multiplication which  are the most fundamental operations  of mental multiplication for most of  these problems  you'll use the addition method but  sometimes you may find it faster  to use subtraction with just a little  bit of practice  you can do any two by one or three by  one problem  in just a few seconds and now would be  an excellent time  to practice with these skills under your  belt  you can even multiply most two-digit  numbers together  we'll have plenty more to say about  mental multiplication techniques in this  course  but before we do that we'll attack the  fourth of the fundamental operations of  arithmetic  so get ready to divide and conquer  [Music]  you
 

